- **Why it holds at 4:** the stock is very cheap against its own history (P/E 11.9x against a 5-year median of 32.3x, an 18.51% FCF yield). The price implies revenue shrinking 2.9% next year. Q3 intrinsic growth was 6.6%, and management reaffirmed 2026 guidance and targets 8–12% growth for 2027–28.
- **Why not 5:** the Brazil decree details and the Italy ruling are still open.
- **Operating margin and ROCE falling:** operating margin fell from 34.3% to 29.6% over 12 months and ROCE from 28.6% to 27.2%. Management blames the Italy and Brazil meal-voucher rules, which fits a regulation-driven reset rather than lost business. ROCE is still well above its FY2021–23 levels and ROIC is flat at 18.5%. If the margin falls below 26%, a trigger sends the stock back for full re-research.
- **Brazil fee cap and interoperability:** the main bear risk is that the decree opens Edenred's closed network and the fee cut becomes permanent. A fourth trigger catches it if the implementing rules impose full interoperability, if guidance is cut, or if another country caps fees.
- **The real and the Selic:** the macro overlay's official data show something milder than your figures. The real is about 3% weaker against the euro over a month, and the Selic was 13.94% in September, down 0.45pp over three months. The overlay doesn't confirm the ~7% post-election fall or the 4 Nov hold at 13.75%, so I couldn't cite them. Its small tilt toward the bear case moved the odds to bull 25%, base 45%, bear 30% (it was 25/50/25).
- **Targets and expected value:** the 12-month targets are 45.00 bull, 33.00 base and 21.15 bear (EUR). The probability-weighted value is still well above the 24.60 close. The base sits below the mechanical fair value of 38.59 because that model leans on FCF flattered by client float.
